## SCALING
=============

---------------------------
WHY DO WE NEED SCALING ?
---------------------------

Sabse pehle ek simple E-commerce Spring Boot Application lete hain jo ek
single Physical Machine (PM) ya Virtual Machine (VM) par deployed hai.

Architecture:

             Users
                |
                |
                v
        ----------------
        | Spring Boot  |
        | Application  |
        ----------------
                |
                |
                v
             Database

Spring Boot application embedded application server (e.g. Tomcat) ke saath
isi Physical/Virtual server par run kar rahi hai.

Ab is server ke paas kuch limited resources hain.



## SERVER RESOURCES
---------------------

CPU              : 2 Cores
RAM              : 4 GB
Disk             : 100 GB
Network Card     : 1 Gbps



## APPLICATION RESOURCES
--------------------------

1: WORKER THREADS :
--------------------

Har incoming HTTP request ko process karne ke liye Tomcat ko ek worker thread
allocate karna padta hai.

Suppose Tomcat configuration:

    Maximum Threads = 200

Matlab Tomcat ke paas maximum 200 worker threads available hain request
processing ke liye.

Tomcat ek time par maximum 200 requests actively process kar sakta hai
(assuming each request occupies one worker thread).

Baaki requests:

    - Accept Queue me wait kar sakti hain
      (OS / Tomcat configuration par depend karta hai)

                OR

    - Overload hone par reject bhi ho sakti hain.



2: DATABASE CONNECTIONS :
-------------------------

Spring Boot application database se communicate karne ke liye
connection pool use karti hai.

Suppose configuration:

    Maximum Pool Size = 100

Matlab ek time par application maximum 100 active database connections
use kar sakti hai.



## INITIALLY EVERYTHING WORKS FINE :
------------------------------------

Suppose application ke sirf

    100 Users

hain.

Traffic bhi kam hai.

Server ke resources sufficient hain.

Application smoothly chal rahi hai.



## COMPANY STARTS GROWING :
---------------------------

Initially:

    Users

        100

Server Traffic

        10 Requests / Second

Server easily handle kar raha tha.


Kuch months baad:

    Users

        10,000

Ab naturally application par incoming HTTP requests bhi badhne lagti hain.

Suppose traffic increase hokar

    500 Requests / Second

ho gayi.

Server ab bhi kaam kar raha hai, lekin CPU utilization aur memory usage
increase hone lagi.


Aur kuch months baad:

    Users

        100,000

Suppose traffic aur badhkar

    2,000 Requests / Second

ho gayi.

Ab actual problems start hoti hain.



## WHAT EXACTLY STARTS FAILING ?
----------------------------------


1. CPU BECOMES A BOTTLENECK
------------------------------

Har incoming HTTP request ko process karne ke liye ek worker thread allocate
hota hai aur wo thread CPU instructions execute karta hai.

Initially:

    Traffic

        10 Requests / Second

    CPU Utilization

        15%

Traffic increase hua:

    Traffic

        500 Requests / Second

    CPU Utilization

        75%

Traffic aur badha:

    Traffic

        2,000 Requests / Second

    CPU Utilization

        100%

Ab CPU continuously busy hai.

Naye requests ko process karne ke liye immediately CPU time available nahi hai.

Result:

    Response Time increase hone lagta hai.

    Latency badhne lagti hai.



2. TOMCAT THREADS GET EXHAUSTED
----------------------------------

Suppose:

    Tomcat Worker Threads = 200

Ek request ko complete hone me

    2 Seconds

lag rahe hain.

Agar ek hi time par

    500 Requests

aa jaati hain,

to kya hoga?

Pehli

    200 Requests

ko worker thread mil jayega.

Baaki

    300 Requests

immediately process nahi ho paayengi.

Wo:

    - Accept Queue me wait karengi
      (agar queue me jagah hai)

                OR

    - Queue bhi full ho gayi to reject ho sakti hain.


## RESULT:
-----------

    High Response Time
            ↓
        Timeouts
            ↓
    5xx Errors (depending on situation)



3. DATABASE CONNECTION POOL GETS EXHAUSTED
---------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    Maximum DB Connections = 100

Ab ek hi time par

    300 Requests

database access karna chahti hain.

Kya application

    300

database connections bana legi?

Nahi.

Sirf

    100

connections available hain.

Baaki requests connection available hone ka wait karengi.

Agar bahut der tak connection available nahi hua,

to request fail bhi ho sakti hai.


## RESULT :
------------

    Slow Queries
            ↓
        Waiting
            ↓
    Connection Timeout



4. RAM STARTS FILLING UP
--------------------------

Har request kuch memory consume karti hai.

Example:

    - Request Objects
    - Response Objects
    - Local Variables
    - JSON Serialization
    - Hibernate Objects
    - Session Data

Initially:

    RAM Usage

        1 GB / 4 GB

Traffic badha:

    RAM Usage

        3.5 GB / 4 GB

Aur badha:

    RAM Usage

        4 GB / 4 GB

Ab JVM ko Garbage Collection frequently run karni padegi.

GC jab memory reclaim karti hai, kuch phases me application threads ko temporarily stop karna pad sakta hai.

Agar memory fir bhi insufficient hui,

to application

    OutOfMemoryError

bhi throw kar sakti hai.



5. NETWORK BANDWIDTH ALSO HAS A LIMIT
----------------------------------------

Suppose server ka Network Interface Card support karta hai

    1 Gbps

Lekin incoming + outgoing traffic ho gaya

    3 Gbps

Impossible.

Network packets queue hone lagenge.

Result:

    Latency increase hogi.

    Throughput degrade hoga.



## FINAL OBSERVATION
---------------------

Ab dhyan se dekho.

Application me koi bug nahi aaya.

Problem sirf itni hai ki

    Existing Workload

            >

    Server Capacity

ho gayi hai.



## WHAT IS THE ROOT CAUSE ?
----------------------------

Root Cause:

Ek single machine ke resources finite (limited) hote hain.

Chahe wo:

    - CPU
    - RAM
    - Network
    - Thread Pool
    - Database Connections

In sab ki ek upper limit hoti hai.

Jaise-jaise traffic increase hota hai,

ye resources gradually saturate hone lagte hain.
